1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
1.1 Research Question
Can recursively self-modeling inferential systems with evolvable cognitive physics sustain open-ended novelty growth beyond representational saturation?

1.2 Core Finding
Negative. Under fixed representational architectures, novelty growth saturates at a ceiling of N_max ≈ 0.15-0.20 despite:

Evolvable inferential laws via Evolution Strategies

Maintained functional utility (U ≈ 0.54)

Healthy output entropy (H ≈ 0.95)

Metastable dynamics (S ≈ 0.85)

1.3 Key Contribution
Empirical demonstration that representational closure imposes a fundamental bound on open-ended inferential differentiation, providing direct justification for pre-representational signal processing (Phase 12).

1.4 Validated Results
Horizon	Divergence	Novelty	Utility	Entropy	Stability
5k	0.534	0.085	—	—	—
10k	0.495	0.141	—	—	—
25k	0.409	0.151	—	—	—
50k	0.334	0.134	0.540	0.950	0.847
2. RESEARCH FRAMEWORK
2.1 Theoretical Foundations
Phase 11 builds on four theoretical pillars:

Pillar	Source	Relevance
Evolution Strategies	Rechenberg (1973)	Parameter optimization without gradients
Novelty Search	Lehman & Stanley (2011)	Behavioral diversity > objective optimization
Open-Ended Evolution	Bedau (2003)	Sustained novelty as ALife criterion
Autopoiesis	Maturana & Varela (1980)	Self-producing system dynamics
2.2 The Central Hypothesis
text
H₀: Evolvable inferential laws within fixed representational architectures 
    can sustain unbounded novelty growth.

H₁: Representational closure imposes a fundamental novelty ceiling 
    regardless of law evolution.
Result: H₀ rejected, H₁ supported (practical, not statistical, significance).

